body:
Ran rot-canary (DEEP, whole repo) against [move-test-gen](https://github.com/mehvetero/move-test-gen). JS/Node codebase, ~1600 lines across 7 scripts + 4 lint rules + eval runner. First run on a non-TheColliery project.

No false positives from the canary. Three confirmed findings, two suspected.

---

## Confirmed

### 1. SIGTERM not handled — temp dir leak on CI timeout

`scripts/check-coverage.mjs:275`

```js
process.on('SIGINT', () => {
    console.log('\nInterrupted — cleaning up temp directory...');
    cleanup();
    process.exit(130);
});
```

SIGINT only. CI runners send SIGTERM on timeout, not SIGINT. When mutation testing times out in CI, the temp directory (`mtg-mutate-*`) stays in `/tmp/`. Verified:

```
$ grep -n 'SIGTERM\|SIGINT' scripts/check-coverage.mjs
274:  // handle SIGINT gracefully
275:  process.on('SIGINT', () => {
```

Zero SIGTERM references in the file. Severity: **MEDIUM** (silent resource leak in CI, not a correctness bug).

---

### 2. walkDir diverged between two files — one has crash protection, the other doesn't

`scripts/check-coverage.mjs:26` vs `scripts/lint.mjs:25`

Same function, copy-pasted, then lint.mjs got a fix that check-coverage.mjs didn't:

```diff
 function walkDir(dir, ext) {
   const results = [];
-  for (const entry of readdirSync(dir, { withFileTypes: true })) {
+  let entries;
+  try { entries = readdirSync(dir, { withFileTypes: true }); } catch { return results; }
+  for (const entry of entries) {
```

lint.mjs wraps `readdirSync` in try/catch to handle inaccessible directories (added in commit `2e746e2` — "skip inaccessible directories in walkDir"). check-coverage.mjs still has the unguarded version — crashes on permission-denied directories.

Severity: **MEDIUM** (duplication that already diverged — the pattern that causes real bugs over time).

---

### 3. WIDE_TYPES defined independently in two files

`rules/mov-002-unchecked-arithmetic.mjs:24` and `scripts/move-parser.mjs:14`

```js
// mov-002
const WIDE_TYPES = new Set(['u128', 'u256']);

// move-parser
const WIDE_SET = new Set(['u128', 'u256']);
```

Same set, different name, two files. If one gets updated (say, adding `u512` when Move gets it), the other silently stays behind.

Severity: **LOW** (duplication, not a bug today).

---

## Suspected

These are real patterns but the practical impact in this codebase is near-zero. Listing for completeness.

- **`findBraceBlock` (move-parser.mjs:224)** counts `{` and `}` in string literals. Move doesn't put braces in strings in practice, so no real-world misparse — but the parser would break on `let s = b"some{thing}";` if it existed.

- **`walkDir` (both files)** follows `isDirectory()` without checking `isSymbolicLink()`. Symlink cycles cause infinite recursion. Move projects don't use symlinks, so untriggerable in practice.

---

## What I didn't find

No false positives from rot-canary on this codebase. The categories that don't apply (async, performance hot paths) correctly produced nothing. The "dead/disconnected" category didn't flag anything — all exports are used, no orphan functions. That's a clean bill on a codebase I know line-by-line.

The three findings are real. #1 and #2 are ones I should have caught during development — #2 especially, since I wrote the fix in one file and forgot to port it to the other.
